course: dictionary
lesson: 15043
title: Reticence: keeping quiet
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reticence is a noun. It means the habit of not saying much, or of keeping your feelings and thoughts to yourself. A person who shows reticence is quiet and private. «Despite his reticence, he was kind.» Context tells you that being reticent is not the same as being unkind: he was quiet, but he still cared about others.
explain: Sometimes reticence means being unwilling to do something. This is the informal meaning, close to reluctance. «She showed reticence about joining the choir.» The word about tells you what she was unwilling to do. Try your meaning in the sentence: would «She showed unwillingness about joining the choir» still make sense? Yes, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Look for clue words. «He overcame his reticence and shared his drawings.» Overcame tells you that reticence was holding him back, and he got past it. When you meet a new word, ask what the words around it are telling you.
fact: Reticence is a noun, meaning the quality or habit of being reticent.
fact: «Reticence» means keeping quiet and private about your thoughts and feelings.
fact: «Reticence» can also mean being unwilling to do something, which is called reluctance.
fact: Common phrases: «reticence about», «his usual reticence», «overcome her reticence».
fact: Reticence is not the same as being rude; a person with reticence may be kind, just quiet.
exercise: «Despite his reticence, he was kind.» What does reticence mean here? | Telling everyone a secret | Being very angry | Being loud and cheerful | * Keeping quiet and not saying much
exercise: «She showed reticence about joining the choir.» What does reticence suggest? | Eagerness to sing solos | * Unwillingness, or reluctance, to join in | Loud singing in the hall | Trouble with money
exercise: «He overcame his reticence and shared his drawings.» What did he do with his reticence? | He sold it to a friend | He added to it | He wrote it down | * He got past it and held nothing back
test: «Mia's reticence meant she rarely spoke in class, but she always listened carefully.» What does reticence mean? | Being loud and bossy | Being very tall | * A habit of not saying much | Telling jokes all day
test: Which sentence uses reticence correctly? | The reticence was loud and cheerful | Reticence ran very fast around the park | * Her reticence about the plan showed she was not keen to join in | Reticence is a bright blue colour
exam: Which sentence best explains reticence? | Running away fast when a dog barks | Being loud and eager to speak | * Keeping quiet and private, or being unwilling to take part | Being very hungry after a long walk
